# Users: who may talk to this store, and what that lets them do.
#
# A store with no user is open, because requiring a signin against an empty one
# locks everybody out of it with no way in to fix that. Declaring the first user
# is what closes it — which is why every case below that needs an open store
# comes before the first `DEFINE USER`.
#
# A corpus file is one session against one store, so these run in order. The
# runner does not sign in, so it is anonymous throughout: what these cases pin
# is the open-store rule and the shape of the statements, and the per-role
# checks are asserted where a session can actually sign in.
#
# One consequence is worth naming: once the store is closed, an anonymous
# session cannot re-open it by dropping the last user. There is no back door,
# which is the point — but it also means a lost owner password is a restore from
# backup rather than a recovery, and that belongs in the operations notes.

--- case: a namespace and a database, to work in
DEFINE NAMESPACE prod;
USE NAMESPACE prod;
DEFINE DATABASE orders;
USE DATABASE orders;
DEFINE COLLECTION notes;
--- expect: ok

--- case: an open store runs anything, which is what makes an empty one usable
CREATE notes:1 = { body: 'before there were users' };
--- expect: ok

--- case: an open store answers the listing, and the list is empty
INFO FOR USERS;
--- expect: ok

--- case: a user is declared like anything else, and its tenancy is resolved
DEFINE USER ada ON prod.orders ROLE editor PASSWORD 'correct horse';
--- expect: ok

--- case: the store is closed now, so an anonymous read is refused
SELECT * FROM notes;
--- expect: error NotSignedIn

--- case: and so is an anonymous write
CREATE notes:2 = { body: 'after' };
--- expect: error NotSignedIn

--- case: an anonymous DEFINE USER is refused too, once there is a user to ask
DEFINE USER grace ROLE viewer PASSWORD 'x';
--- expect: error NotSignedIn

--- case: and so is dropping one, so a store cannot be re-opened from outside
DROP USER ada;
--- expect: error NotSignedIn


--- case: and the listing is refused once the store is closed, like every read
INFO FOR USERS;
--- expect: error NotSignedIn

--- case: altering a user is administering one, so an anonymous caller is refused
ALTER USER ada SET ROLE viewer;
--- expect: error NotSignedIn

--- case: and so is changing a password, which is the same act on a different field
ALTER USER ada SET PASSWORD 'a different horse';
--- expect: error NotSignedIn

--- case: the verb takes USER and says so rather than guessing what was meant
ALTER ada SET ROLE viewer;
--- expect: error UnexpectedToken

--- case: and it changes one named thing, never a whole record at once
ALTER USER ada SET everything;
--- expect: error UnexpectedToken
